` → throws (connection gate) for actions that carry connectionMode (open, command).\n- For click/type with external provenance: the tool must call `status` first (session-ownership preflight) and deny when the session is non-managed.\n- For navigate/status/waitForSelector/screenshot/getConsoleMessages with external provenance and a managed/absent connection: allowed and does NOT perform a status preflight (so click/type only get the extra gate).\n- For evaluate/command/close with external provenance: they should NOT perform the session-ownership preflight → recorded residual.\n\nHold on: `command` carries `connectionMode` in payload, so it passes the connection-mode gate. `evaluate` and `close` don't carry connectionMode. So AC3 says \"navigate/status/waitForSelector/screenshot/getConsoleMessages only pass connection-mode gate\" — right. And AC4: evaluate/command/close are side-effectful but NOT wired to session-ownership gate.\n\nI can verify \"not wired\" behaviorally: for external provenance, `evaluate`/`close` on a non-managed session are allowed (no preflight status call). For `command`, connection gate applies but not session gate.\n\nThis behavioral approach is best: it exercises `createAgentBrowserTool` with a fake invoker that records calls. Let me design it.\n\nThe tool's execute for click/type calls `assertSessionEffectAllowed` which calls `params.invoke(\"status\", {})` and reads `result.sessions[].connection.mode`. If it denies, throws ToolAuthorizationError. Good.\n\nFor actions like `click`, after preflight passes it calls `options.invoke(action, payload)`.\n\nLet me define a fake invoker:\n```ts\nfunction makeInvoker(overrides) {\n  const calls: Array<{action:string; payload:any}> = [];\n  const invoke = async (action, payload) => {\n    calls.push({action, payload});\n    if (action === 'status') return { success: true, sessions: [{ id: payloadSessionIdOrSomething }] };\n    ...\n  };\n}\n```\nBut `status` payload is `{}` — the tool calls `invoke(\"