exact output summary. Record vitest/lint/typecheck/comms as `not-run` (dependencies absent) with that reason — do NOT claim PASS.\n\nBut the TASK acceptance criterion #6 says all those must pass. If I mark them not-run, does the task count as complete? The task instruction overrides: \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". So not-run with reason is acceptable and honest. I'll set status `completed` for the implementation, but be explicit about unrun gates.\n\nHmm, the RESULT_SCHEMA requires status ∈ {completed, blocked} and testsRun (array of strings). So testsRun should list the actual commands I ran. Let me keep the offline probe reproducible by keeping a dedicated script in the repo tracked path? The targetScope is fixed to 5 files — adding an extra tracked file outside scope might violate `ownedPaths`/`touchedAreas` ownership in harness validate (changed-path ownership). Since there's no git repo, harness validate with diff can't run (no git). I ran `--no-diff`. So ownership isn't checked here anyway. But the oracle may run harness validate too.\n\nI'll keep the probe transient and delete it, citing it as an executed local check. Actually to make it reproducible and honest, I can put the probe logic INSIDE the deliverable test file as the primary gate (vitest) AND run an equivalent offline Node probe. The offline probe is just my verification method; I'll describe it in the acceptance JSON as a local capability probe, and note files were transient.\n\nHmm, simpler: I'll keep the probe but not as a tracked deliverable — place it under `artifacts/` ? That changes scope. Let me just delete it and be transparent: \"Executed an offline Node type-stripping probe (transient scratch; deleted after run) that imported the real modules...\"\n\nActually, I realize I can make the check reproducible without vitest by writing the assertions in the test file AND ALSO providing the same assertions in a plain-node-friendly way... no.\n\nLet me reconsider: Is vitest truly unavailable? `node_modules` absent, and no network → cannot install. Correct, unavailable.\n\nDecision: \n- Deliverable: 5 scoped files.\n- Run: harness validate (--no-diff), harness context, harness run --dry-run, credential scan (grep-based), SHA256SUMS compute+verify, and the offline Node probe on the real policy modules. Delete probe afterward.\n- Acceptance JSON: honest statuses.\n\nNow, the dry-run prints \"Harness result: pass\" and writes a report file into `harness/reports/`? Let me check writeReport path — it writes to some reports dir. That would create files outside my scope! Let me check.\n\n`report.mjs`: writes markdownPath. Let me look.\n\nLet me check report.mjs.\n\nAlso harness `run --dry-run` selected steps are `skipped` and `result` = pass (failures empty, residual empty). But dry-run still writes a report file. I need to know where — if it writes into tracked `reports/`, that might be fine (reports/ exists). But changing tracked files beyond scope... Let me check.\n\nLet me read report.mjs.\n\nAlso, I should double check: does validate with `--no-diff` still pass for my new spec? It requires scenario spec exists (`gateway-backend-communication`), requiredProfiles include fast & comms, requiredRules include the four scenario rules, touchedAreas non-empty, expectedUserBehavior non-empty, acceptance non-empty, docs.required boolean. And `validateSpecPortability` forbids machine-local paths. Good.\n\nLet me read report.mjs to see the output location.\n\nLet me proceed. First read report.mjs."}}
