{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_255","type":"reasoning","text":"harness run writes into artifacts/harness/latest.json. That's an artifact directory - it's fine, likely gitignored. Let me check .gitignore.\n\nNow let me create the deliverables. Target scope:\n1. docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/s04-browser-effect-boundary.md\n2. tests/unit/browser-effect-boundary-ledger.test.ts\n3. harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-browser-effect-boundary.md\n4. docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/s04-acceptance.json\n5. docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/S04-SHA256SUMS\n\nBut the acceptance criteria mention tests running. Since we cannot run vitest (no deps), we must be honest. The acceptance criteria says \"干净候选定向测试、类型、scoped lint、harness validate/dry-run、comms replay/compare 通过\". But we can't install deps. The instruction says \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". So I must run what's runnable and honestly report which gates could not be run (no deps installed, no git repo).\n\nLet me check .gitignore for artifacts/harness.\n\nLet me also think about the test design. S04 is a verification/ledger packet like S02/S03: a per-action coverage ledger + residual for evaluate/command/close that are side-effectful but NOT wired to session-ownership gate. The test should:\n- Assert the pure policy matrix (external/derived-untrusted/missing/invalid blocked for auto-connect/cdp/external-profile; managed allowed; trusted system/direct operator not gated) — I verified this runs.\n- Assert click/type require managed session under external/unknown (verified).\n- Assert per-action coverage: navigate/status/waitForSelector/screenshot/getConsoleMessages only pass connection-mode gate; click/type additionally session-ownership gate. Need to verify by reading agent-browser-tool.ts: which actions go through assertSessionEffectAllowed (only click/type), which actions carry connectionMode in payload (open, command). Hmm, wait—\"navigate/status/waitForSelector/screenshot/getConsoleMessages 只过连接模式闸门\". Actually the connection-mode gate applies to the payload.connectionMode which only open and command payloads contain connectionMode. navigate/wait/status/screenshot/getConsoleMessages don't carry connectionMode, so the external-access policy effectively can't restrict them (connectionMode undefined → allowed). So the ledger records: for these actions, the connection-mode gate is present in the tool but there's no external mode to select; they're not session-ownership-gated.\n\nLet me design the test as a source-reading ledger test (like S02's residual ledger test) that:\n1. Pins the pure policy matrix by importing the real policy + provenance.\n2. Reads agent-browser-tool.ts to assert:\n   - `assertSessionEffectAllowed` is called exactly for click/type (i.e., the guard block `if (action === \"click\" || action === \"type\")`), \n   - `resolveAgentBrowserExternalAccessPolicy` is invoked for all actions (call site, unconditionally).\n   - evaluate/command/close are NOT in the session-effect preflight → residual.\n3. A ledger doc coverage check.\n\nFor a robust \"per-action coverage\" test that's executable without deps, the best is a hybrid: import real modules for policy matrix; and for tool wiring, read source text + assert exact literals (the same approach S02 uses for residual egress). That's genuinely meaningful and runs offline.\n\nActually better: I can *execute* the real tool! Importing agent-browser-tool.ts requires `typebox` and `../schema/string-enum.js` and `./common.js`. typebox is a dependency — not available offline. So I cannot import the tool. Hmm. But I verified the policy imports fine (only imports shared provenance). So the executable part is the policy; the tool wiring part must be source-text based.\n\nThat's consistent with S02's approach (source-text pinning). Good.\n\nLet me check if `tests/unit` tests can import the policy via relative path `../../openclaw_runtime/src/agents/tools/agent-browser-external-access-policy`. Under vitest config, the runtime alias plugin handles @openclaw/*; relative path imports work. The policy imports `../../../../shared/security/content-provenance.js` — relative with .js. Vitest/Vite resolves .js→.ts? Actually Vite by default does not resolve .js→.ts for relative imports... but many existing tests import runtime modules with .js internal imports and they work (e.g., message-secret-read-policy.test.ts imports `./message-secret-read-policy.js` and the runtime run-vitest likely has extensions resolution). For the root vitest config, the runtime modules are loaded via the alias plugin only for @openclaw/* bare specifiers. Relative `.js` imports inside runtime... Vite's resolver: when importing a file that doesn't exist as .js, Vite tries extensions? Vite does have `resolve.extensions` defaulting to ['.mjs','.js','.mts','.ts','.jsx','.tsx','.json'] but that only applies to extensionless imports. For an explicit `.js` that doesn't exist, Vite... actually Vite does try to resolve `.js` to `.ts`? There's a known behavior: Vite resolves `./foo.js` to `./foo.ts`? No — that's TypeScript's behavior. Vite added support: \"resolve.js to .ts\" — I recall Vite does support importing .ts via .js specifier since it mimics TS. Yes, Vite supports this since it processes TS. Actually Vite's resolver has a special case: if the importer is a TS file and the import ends in .js, it tries the .ts/.tsx variant. This is true (`vite:resolve` `isFromTsImporter`). So it works.\n\nGood — so a test importing the policy relative path will work under root vitest. I'll still keep the executable import approach (matching the repo's existing pattern in message-secret-read-policy.test.ts which imports policy via `./....js`).\n\nWait — my test is at tests/unit/browser-effect-boundary-ledger.test.ts. I'll import:\n- `../../shared/security/content-provenance` (no ext, like external-content-provenance.test.ts)\n- `../../openclaw_runtime/src/agents/tools/agent-browser-external-access-policy` (no ext)\n\nNow to reproduce \"verified\" I ran the policy through node type-stripping, which validates the logic. Good enough as evidence that the test's assertions match reality.\n\nNow design test file content. Let me write it.\n\nAssertions:\nA. Connection-mode gate (all actions): for external/derived/invalid/missing provenance, connectionMode ∈ {auto-connect, cdp, external-profile} → allowed=false; managed → allowed=true. Trusted system & operator → allowed=true for all modes.\nB. Session-effect gate (click/type): for external/unknown, sessionConnectionMode==='managed' → allowed=true (restricted=true); other/undefined → allowed=false. For trusted/operator → always allowed (restricted=false).\nC. `shouldPreflightAgentBrowserSessionEffect` true for external/derived/missing/invalid, false for trusted/operator.\nD. Wiring: source asserts `resolveAgentBrowserExternalAccessPolicy(` invoked once, unconditionally, and its result gates via throw; `if (action === \"click\" || action === \"type\")` is the ONLY session-effect call; the preflight list is exactly [\"click\",\"type\"].\nE. Per-action coverage classification: define a ledger in the test mapping each action to covered gates, and cross-check against source: \n   - connection-mode gate applies to all actions (gate present in code, executed before bridge).\n   - session-ownership gate applies only to click/type.\n   - evaluate/command/close are side-effectful but NOT session-ownership gated → residual.\nF. Residual: assert `evaluate`, `command`, `close` do NOT appear in the session-effect preflight condition, and the doc records them as residual.\nG. Ledger doc exists and mentions each action + residual.\nH. Not weakened: SSRF/private-network/kill-switch unchanged — assert the browser private-network default is deny unless explicitly allowed (read config.ts) and that the policy module doesn't touch SSRF/kill switch. And assert no third policy: only one policy file exports these, and the tool imports from it.\n\nFor \"not weakened\" I can read `openclaw_runtime/extensions/browser/src/browser/config.ts` and assert the default resolveBrowserSsrFPolicy returns `{}` (fail-closed) when nothing explicit, and the private-network allow requires explicit true. Also assert `agent-browser-external-access-policy.ts` text doesn't reference ssrf/killSwitch/privateNetwork. And assertion that tool still requires `allowExternalBrowser` (doesn't bypass Main validation): assert `allowExternalBrowser` still in the schema & payload (source read), and the policy never sets it.\n\nLet me also assert \"no third policy\": tool imports exactly the existing three symbols from the one policy module; and SENSITIVE_SINK_CLASSIFICATION browser-effect enforcedAt has exactly 2 points both pointing at that same policy file with those two exported symbols (reuses existing classification).\n\nLet me write the file now. Need to be careful about placeholders and encoding (UTF-8, no BOM). I'll use apply_patch.\n\nLet me first check the private-network default assertion source lines exactly.\n\nconfig.ts lines 284-315 as shown: when not resolvedAllowPrivateNetwork and no explicit setting and no allowedHostnames/hostnameAllowlist → returns {} (keeps default fail-closed). Good.\n\nAlso there's a `dangerouslyAllowPrivateNetwork` gate. Also `requiresInspectableBrowserNavigationRedirects(ssrfPolicy)` in navigation-guard. Let me read navigation-guard + ssrf.ts to get a precise \"not weakened\" anchor.\n\nActually I should keep \"not weakened\" checks minimal and grounded: \n- read config.ts, assert `return {}` present and the private-network allow requires explicit true.\n- read navigation-guard.ts, assert redirect inspection still required for default policy (fail-closed).\n\nLet me read those two precisely."}}
